We sincerely thank all reviewers and the area chair for their detailed feedback and constructive suggestions. In this rebuttal, we have substantially strengthened our paper by:

(1) providing new analyses on unseen-class behavior, expert specialization, in-context exemplar sensitivity, extended discussion of 3D extensibility, and model interpretability, including detailed evaluations of how reference quality and anatomical/domain shifts affect Mode-2 performance;

(2) adding multiple new experiments, including robustness to exemplar selection, few-shot reference scaling, throughput and computational efficiency measurements under different MoE configurations, and additional comparisons with MedSAM and specialist nnU-Net models;

 (3) offering deeper explanation of our unified design, including why the dual-prompt formulation and MoE decoder naturally support 3D extension, how the architecture mitigates cross-mode interference, and the clinical relevance of integrating semantic, in-context, and interactive segmentation within one unified framework;

(4) revising the manuscript to improve clarity, expand discussion, and incorporate visual examples, new tables, and additional results in the appendix.

We appreciate the reviewers’ thoughtful comments, which have significantly improved the quality, completeness, and practical grounding of our work. We hope the provided evidence and clarifications satisfactorily address all concerns, and we thank the reviewers and AC again for their time and insightful evaluation.
